# shir_nodes/string_trim.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Trim leading/trailing whitespace (lowered from `sed`, `xargs`).
#
# Backends: Perl s/^\s+//, JS trim(), C isspace loop, Rust .trim()
# Fallback: sh2.stringTrim(text, leading, trailing)

node StringTrim
tag "StringTrim"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field leading: bool
field trailing: bool
# RESOLVED — the value is a trim: drop ONE RUN of the measured whitespace class at each
# enabled end, and nothing else. All of it is proved in TextOps#string_trim_sound (a
# bundle; a bare "node = definition" equation would be `refl` and would prove nothing):
#  * FLAGS: the four flag pairs are `s` (neither), `lead_drop` (leading only),
#    `trail_drop` (trailing only) and `trail_drop ∘ lead_drop` (both) — the two ends are
#    independent, which is what four of the six renderers get wrong;
#  * IDEMPOTENT: trimming again changes nothing, for each flag pair that can be one
#    (TextOps#trim_by_idem_both / _pre / _suf, at the node's name);
#  * RUN, NOT FILTER: the bytes removed at each end are all IN the class and the interior
#    is untouched — `∃a b. s = a @ string_trim True True s @ b` with every byte of `a` and
#    of `b` in `is_ws` (TextOps#string_trim_removes_runs). A filter, or a one-byte strip,
#    fails exactly here;
#  * BOUNDED: a trim invents no byte (`set ⊆ set`) and never lengthens (`length ≤ length`).
# NON-VACUITY: TextOps#string_trim_witness — space/tab/`a`/space trims to `a`, an
# all-whitespace string to empty, each one-sided trim keeps the far end, and a string whose
# ends are already outside the class is unchanged.
# REFUTED: a one-byte strip per side (TextOps#trim_one_is_not_the_node) and "trim" as a
# filter of the class over the whole string (TextOps#filtering_is_not_trimming).
# NOT PROMISED (outside this model): the whitespace CLASS is not the node's — each
# renderer's class is measured and tabulated in ShIR_TextOps_Space, so a renderer that uses
# a different class denotes a different function (that is a renderer gap with its own
# witnesses, not a gap in this obligation), and non-ASCII casing/whitespace beyond those
# tables is out of model.
spec: TextOps#string_trim_sound
